\begin{abstract}
The elementary fact that $\bigcap_{n\ge 0}(2^n) = 0$ in $\ZZ$ is the shadow of a general theory. For a commutative domain $R$ and an element $a \in R$ we study the \emph{principal $a$-adic filtration} $n \mapsto (a^n)$ and call it \emph{separated} when $\bigcap_n (a^n) = 0$. We prove two \emph{a priori} independent sufficient conditions for separation: a chain condition (well-founded divisibility, which covers all Noetherian domains and all unique factorization domains) applied to a non-unit $a$, and a \emph{height condition} asserting merely the existence of some function $v : R \to \NN$ with $v(x) < v(ax)$ for all $x \ne 0$. Our main structural theorem is that over a domain the height condition is not only sufficient but necessary: separation holds if and only if such a height function exists, and the $a$-adic order $\ord_a$ is the pointwise minimal height function. Consequently the Krull-type intersection theorem is fundamentally a statement about the existence of an $\NN$-valued height, with chain conditions serving only as a device for producing one. We complement this with three further characterisations --- the intersection $J = \bigcap_n(a^n)$ is the greatest fixed point of $I \mapsto (a)\cdot I$ on the ideal lattice; separation is equivalent to Hausdorffness of the $a$-adic topology; separation is equivalent to injectivity of the map into the $a$-adic completion --- and with the construction, for prime $a$, of a genuine non-archimedean absolute value $\|x\|_a = 2^{-\ord_a(x)}$. Finally we delimit the theory sharply: in the classical $D + M$ domain $S = \ZZ + X\,\QQ[X]$ the element $2$ is a nonzero non-unit with $\bigcap_n(2^n) = X\QQ[X] \neq 0$, so that no height function for $2$ exists on $S$ and $S$ is neither Noetherian nor a UFD; and the polynomial ring $\QQ[X_0, X_1, \dots]$ shows that the chain-condition hypothesis is strictly more general than the Noetherian one.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Krull intersection theorem, principal filtration, adic topology, height function, multiplicity, non-archimedean absolute value, Nakayama's lemma, $D+M$ construction.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The motivating fact}

Every student of arithmetic knows that no nonzero integer is divisible by all powers of $2$: if $2^n \mid m$ and $m \ne 0$ then $2^n \le |m|$, which fails once $n$ exceeds $\log_2|m|$. In ideal-theoretic language,
\[
  \bigcap_{n \ge 0} (2^n) = 0 \qquad \text{in } \ZZ.
\]
The statement is so elementary that it is easy to miss how much it encodes. It is the assertion that the $2$-adic valuation on $\ZZ$ is finite on nonzero elements; equivalently, that the $2$-adic absolute value is nondegenerate; equivalently, that the $2$-adic topology on $\ZZ$ is Hausdorff; equivalently, that $\ZZ$ embeds in $\ZZ_2$.

\subsection{The generalisation and the question}

Replace $\ZZ$ by a commutative domain $R$ and $2$ by an arbitrary $a \in R$. The filtration $n \mapsto (a^n)$ still descends; when does it descend to zero? The two obvious obstructions --- $a$ a unit (filtration constant, $=R$), and the trivial ring --- are easily excluded. The nontrivial question is whether the remaining hypothesis ``$a$ is a nonzero non-unit'' suffices. It does not, and the whole content of the theory lies in identifying what does.

Two answers are classical in spirit. The first is Krull-flavoured and imposes finiteness on $R$. The second is valuation-flavoured and imposes nothing on $R$ beyond the existence of a measuring device. Our main contribution is that over a domain the second is \emph{equivalent} to separation, so it subsumes the first conceptually: chain conditions are merely one mechanism for producing heights.

\section{The principal filtration and separation}

\begin{definition}[Principal filtration]
Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $a \in R$. The \emph{principal $a$-adic filtration} is the family of ideals
\[
  \filt_a(n) = (a^n) = \{a^n y : y \in R\}, \qquad n \in \NN.
\]
\end{definition}

Two immediate remarks. First, $\filt_a(0) = (1) = R$. Second, $\filt_a(n) = (a)^n$: the $n$-th power of the principal ideal $(a)$ is the principal ideal generated by $a^n$. So the filtration is exactly the power filtration of the ideal $(a)$.

\begin{lemma}[Antitonicity]
If $m \le n$ then $\filt_a(n) \subseteq \filt_a(m)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$x \in \filt_a(n)$ means $a^n \mid x$, and $a^m \mid a^n$ when $m \le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Separation]
The principal filtration at $a$ is \emph{separated} if $\bigcap_{n \in \NN} (a^n) = 0$. We write $\Sep(a)$ for this property.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Membership in the intersection]\label{lem:mem}
$x \in \bigcap_n (a^n)$ if and only if $a^n \mid x$ for every $n$. Consequently
\[
  \Sep(a) \iff \bigl(\forall x \in R:\ (\forall n,\ a^n \mid x) \Rightarrow x = 0\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Membership in an intersection of ideals is membership in each, and membership in the principal ideal $(a^n)$ is divisibility by $a^n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Degenerate elements}

\begin{proposition}[Zero is separated]\label{prop:zero}
$\Sep(0)$ holds in every commutative ring.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $0^n \mid x$ for all $n$, take $n = 1$: then $0 \mid x$, i.e.\ $x = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Units are never separated]\label{prop:unit}
If $a$ is a unit then $\bigcap_n (a^n) = R$; hence in a nontrivial ring $\Sep(a)$ fails.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$a^n$ is a unit, so $a^n \mid x$ for every $x$, giving $\bigcap_n(a^n) = R$. If this were $0$ then $1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Criterion I: heights, with no chain condition}

\begin{definition}[Height function]
Let $a \in R$. A function $v : R \to \NN$ is a \emph{height function for $a$} if
\[
  v(x) < v(ax) \qquad \text{for every } x \in R \setminus \{0\}.
\]
\end{definition}

No further structure is demanded: $v$ need not be additive, monotone under divisibility, or canonical. It must merely strictly increase along multiplication by $a$, off zero.

\begin{lemma}[Linear growth along the filtration]\label{lem:growth}
Let $R$ be a domain, $a \ne 0$, and $v$ a height function for $a$. Then for all $n \in \NN$ and all $x \ne 0$,
\[
  n + v(x) \le v(a^n x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For $n = 0$ the claim is $v(x) \le v(x)$. Assume $n + v(x) \le v(a^n x)$. Since $R$ is a domain and $a \ne 0 \ne x$, we have $a^n x \ne 0$, so the height property applies to it: $v(a^n x) < v(a\,(a^n x)) = v(a^{n+1} x)$. Combining, $(n+1) + v(x) \le v(a^n x) + 1 \le v(a^{n+1}x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Height Criterion]\label{thm:height}
Let $R$ be a domain and $a \in R$. If there exists a height function for $a$, then the principal filtration at $a$ is separated.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $a = 0$, apply Proposition~\ref{prop:zero}. Assume $a \ne 0$ and let $v$ be a height function. Let $x$ be divisible by every power of $a$ and suppose $x \ne 0$. Put $n = v(x) + 1$ and write $x = a^n y$. If $y = 0$ then $x = 0$, a contradiction; so $y \ne 0$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:growth} gives
\[
  v(x) = v(a^n y) \ge n + v(y) = v(x) + 1 + v(y) \ge v(x) + 1,
\]
which is impossible. Hence $x = 0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Classical instances, immediately}

\begin{corollary}[Integers]\label{cor:int}
For $a \in \ZZ$ with $|a| \ge 2$, $\bigcap_n (a^n) = 0$. In particular $\bigcap_n(2^n) = 0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $v(x) = |x|$. For $x \ne 0$, $|ax| = |a|\,|x| \ge 2|x| > |x|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Polynomials]
For $k$ a domain, $\bigcap_n (X^n) = 0$ in $k[X]$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $v(p) = \deg p$ (with $\deg 0 = 0$). For $p \ne 0$, $\deg(Xp) = \deg p + 1$.
\end{proof}

Neither proof used unique factorization, Noetherianity, or any finiteness of $R$.

\section{Criterion II: chain conditions and the Krull intersection theorem}

\begin{definition}[Finite multiplicity]
For $a, x \in R$, say $x$ has \emph{finite $a$-multiplicity} if there is some $n$ with $a^n \nmid x$; in that case the \emph{$a$-adic order} $\ord_a(x)$ is the greatest $n$ with $a^n \mid x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Separation $=$ finite multiplicity]\label{prop:finmult}
$\Sep(a)$ holds if and only if every nonzero $x \in R$ has finite $a$-multiplicity.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If some $x \ne 0$ had infinite multiplicity then $a^n \mid x$ for all $n$, so $x = 0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:mem} --- contradiction. ($\Leftarrow$) If $a^n \mid x$ for all $n$ and $x \ne 0$, then $x$ has infinite multiplicity, contradicting the hypothesis; so $x = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Well-founded divisibility]
A domain $R$ is \emph{well founded for divisibility} if there is no infinite sequence $(x_i)_{i \in \NN}$ of nonzero elements in which each $x_{i+1}$ properly divides $x_i$ --- ``properly'' meaning $x_{i+1} \mid x_i$ but $x_i \nmid x_{i+1}$.
\end{definition}

Every Noetherian domain is well founded for divisibility (a strictly increasing chain of principal ideals would contradict the ascending chain condition), and so is every unique factorization domain (the number of irreducible factors strictly decreases). The two classes are incomparable: UFDs need not be Noetherian (Section~\ref{sec:beyond}).

\begin{theorem}[Krull separation, principal case]\label{thm:krull}
Let $R$ be a domain, well founded for divisibility, and let $a \in R$ be a non-unit. Then the principal filtration at $a$ is separated.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:finmult} it suffices to show every nonzero $x$ has finite $a$-multiplicity. Suppose not: $a^n \mid x$ for all $n$, with $x \ne 0$. Write $x = a^n y_n$. Because $a$ is a non-unit and $R$ is a domain, $y_{n+1}$ properly divides $y_n$ for every $n$ (indeed $y_n = a y_{n+1}$, and if conversely $y_{n+1} \mid y_n$ then $a$ would be a unit by cancellation). This is an infinite properly descending divisibility chain, contradicting well-foundedness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation in the well-founded case]\label{thm:char}
Let $R$ be a nontrivial domain, well founded for divisibility. Then for every $a \in R$: $\Sep(a) \iff a$ is not a unit.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Proposition~\ref{prop:unit}. ($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:krull}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Noetherian case]\label{cor:noeth}
In a Noetherian domain, $\bigcap_n(a^n) = 0$ for every non-unit $a$; i.e.\ $\bigcap_n I^n = 0$ for every proper principal ideal $I$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[UFD case]
In a unique factorization domain, $\bigcap_n (a^n) = 0$ for every non-unit $a$. Since UFDs need not be Noetherian, this is not contained in Corollary~\ref{cor:noeth}.
\end{corollary}

\section{The two criteria coincide}

\begin{lemma}[The order increments by one]\label{lem:incr}
Let $R$ be a domain, $a \ne 0$, and suppose $x$ and $ax$ both have finite $a$-multiplicity. Then $\ord_a(ax) = \ord_a(x) + 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Put $m = \ord_a(x)$ and write $x = a^m c$. Then $ax = a^{m+1} c$, so $\ord_a(ax) \ge m+1$. Conversely suppose $a^{m+2} \mid ax$, say $ax = a^{m+2}c'$. Cancelling the nonzero $a$ (valid in a domain) gives $x = a^{m+1}c'$, so $m + 1 \le \ord_a(x) = m$ --- absurd. Hence $\ord_a(ax) = m+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The order is a height function]\label{cor:ordheight}
If $\Sep(a)$ holds in a domain with $a \ne 0$, then $v = \ord_a$ satisfies $v(x) < v(ax)$ for all $x \ne 0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:finmult} both $x$ and $ax$ (nonzero since $R$ is a domain) have finite multiplicity, so Lemma~\ref{lem:incr} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation is exactly the existence of a height]\label{thm:equiv}
Let $R$ be a domain and $a \in R$. Then
\[
  \Sep(a) \iff \exists\, v : R \to \NN \text{ with } v(x) < v(ax) \text{ for all } x \ne 0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:height}. ($\Rightarrow$) If $a \ne 0$, take $v = \ord_a$ and apply Corollary~\ref{cor:ordheight}. If $a = 0$, take the indicator $v(x) = 1$ if $x = 0$ and $v(x) = 0$ otherwise: for $x \ne 0$ we have $v(x) = 0 < 1 = v(0\cdot x)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} is the conceptual centre of the paper. A Krull-type intersection theorem for a \emph{principal} filtration is never really about ascending chains; chains enter only as one way of exhibiting an $\NN$-valued height.

\section{The intersection as a greatest fixed point}

\begin{definition}[$a$-divisible ideal]
An ideal $I \subseteq R$ is \emph{$a$-divisible} if $I \subseteq (a)\cdot I$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Both inclusions]\label{lem:fix}
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Any commutative ring.) If $I$ is $a$-divisible then $I \subseteq \bigcap_n (a^n)$.
\item (Domains.) $J = \bigcap_n(a^n)$ is $a$-divisible.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(1) By induction, $I \subseteq (a^n)$ for all $n$: trivially for $n = 0$; and if $I \subseteq (a^n)$ then $I \subseteq (a)I \subseteq (a)(a^n) = (a^{n+1})$.
(2) If $a = 0$ then $J = 0$. Let $a \ne 0$ and $x \in J$; write $x = a y$. We claim $y \in J$: for each $n$, write $x = a^{n+1}c$; then $ay = a\,(a^n c)$, and cancelling $a$ gives $y = a^n c$. Hence $x = ay \in (a)J$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Greatest fixed point]\label{thm:gfp}
Let $R$ be a domain and $a \in R$. Then $J = \bigcap_n(a^n)$ satisfies $J = (a)\cdot J$ and is the greatest element of the set of $a$-divisible ideals ordered by inclusion.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:fix}(2) gives $J \subseteq (a)J$; the reverse holds since $(a)J \subseteq J$ always. Lemma~\ref{lem:fix}(1) gives maximality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Separation via fixed points]\label{cor:gfp}
Over a domain, $\Sep(a)$ holds if and only if the only ideal $I$ with $I \subseteq (a)\,I$ is $I = 0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If $\Sep(a)$ and $I \subseteq (a)I$ then $I \subseteq J = 0$. Conversely $J$ itself is $a$-divisible, so the hypothesis forces $J = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nakayama proof of the Noetherian case]
Let $R$ be a Noetherian domain and $a$ a non-unit. Then $\Sep(a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $J = \bigcap_n(a^n)$. Since $R$ is Noetherian, $J$ is finitely generated, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:gfp}, $J \subseteq (a)\cdot J$. The determinant-trick form of Nakayama's lemma yields $r \in R$ with $r - 1 \in (a)$ and $r\,x = 0$ for all $x \in J$. Write $r - 1 = a t$. If $r = 0$ then $a\,(-t) = 1$, contradicting that $a$ is a non-unit. Hence $r \ne 0$, and since $R$ is a domain, $rx = 0$ with $x \in J$ forces $x = 0$. Thus $J = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minimality of the $a$-adic order]\label{thm:min}
Let $R$ be a domain, $a \ne 0$, suppose $\Sep(a)$, and let $v$ be any height function for $a$. Then $\ord_a(x) \le v(x)$ for every $x \ne 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $m = \ord_a(x)$ and write $x = a^m c$ with $c \ne 0$. Lemma~\ref{lem:growth} gives $m + v(c) \le v(a^m c) = v(x)$, hence $m \le v(x)$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\ord_a$ is the universal (smallest) witness to separation.

\section{Valuation, absolute value, topology, completion}

Throughout this section $R$ is a domain, $a \in R$, and $\Sep(a)$ holds, so $\ord_a(x) \in \NN$ is defined for every $x \ne 0$.

\begin{lemma}[Divisibility test]\label{lem:divtest}
For $x \ne 0$ and $k \in \NN$: $k \le \ord_a(x) \iff a^k \mid x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity for prime $a$]\label{thm:add}
If $a$ is prime and $x, y \ne 0$, then $\ord_a(xy) = \ord_a(x) + \ord_a(y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The inequality $\ge$ is clear. For $\le$, write $m = \ord_a x$, $n = \ord_a y$, $x = a^m u$, $y = a^n w$ with $a \nmid u$, $a \nmid w$. Then $xy = a^{m+n} uw$, and since $a$ is prime, $a \nmid uw$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ultrametric inequality for the order]\label{thm:ultra}
For $x, y, x+y$ all nonzero, $\min\{\ord_a(x), \ord_a(y)\} \le \ord_a(x+y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $k$ be the minimum. By Lemma~\ref{lem:divtest}, $a^k \mid x$ and $a^k \mid y$, hence $a^k \mid x+y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Adic absolute value]
Set $\|x\|_a = 2^{-\ord_a(x)}$ for $x \ne 0$ and $\|0\|_a = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The $a$-adic absolute value]\label{thm:abs}
Let $R$ be a domain and $a$ a prime element with $\Sep(a)$. Then $\|\cdot\|_a : R \to \RR$ is an absolute value:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\|x\|_a \ge 0$, with $\|x\|_a = 0$ iff $x = 0$;
\item $\|xy\|_a = \|x\|_a\,\|y\|_a$;
\item $\|x+y\|_a \le \max\{\|x\|_a, \|y\|_a\} \le \|x\|_a + \|y\|_a$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover $\|a\|_a = 1/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) Positivity is clear for $x \ne 0$; the vanishing criterion is exactly separation, which guarantees $\ord_a(x)$ is a finite natural number for $x \ne 0$. (2) is Theorem~\ref{thm:add} exponentiated, with zero cases trivial. (3) If any of $x, y, x+y$ vanishes the claim follows from nonnegativity; otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:ultra} and monotonicity of $t \mapsto 2^{-t}$ give the maximum bound, and $\max\{s,t\} \le s+t$ for nonnegative reals. Finally $\ord_a(a) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The nondegeneracy in (1) is exactly separation. If separation failed, some nonzero $x$ would be divisible by all powers of $a$ and any consistent assignment would give $\|x\|_a = 0$, destroying the metric.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
Taking $R = \ZZ$, $a = 2$ recovers the $2$-adic absolute value with $\|2\|_2 = 1/2$. Taking $R = k[X]$, $a = X$ over a field $k$ recovers the order of vanishing at the origin.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Topological characterisation]\label{thm:top}
Give $R$ the $a$-adic topology, in which $\{(a^n)\}_n$ is a neighbourhood basis of $0$. The following are equivalent: (1) $\Sep(a)$; (2) the only element congruent to $0$ modulo every $(a^n)$ is $0$; (3) the $a$-adic topology on $R$ is Hausdorff.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1)$\iff$(2) restates Lemma~\ref{lem:mem}. In a topological group defined by a descending neighbourhood basis of subgroups, Hausdorffness is equivalent to triviality of the intersection of the basis, giving (2)$\iff$(3).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Completion characterisation]\label{thm:compl}
$\Sep(a)$ holds if and only if the canonical map $R \to \varprojlim_n R/(a^n)$ into the $a$-adic completion is injective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The kernel consists of those $x$ vanishing in $R/(a^n)$ for all $n$, i.e.\ $x \in \bigcap_n(a^n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
In a domain well founded for divisibility, every non-unit $a$ yields a Hausdorff $a$-adic topology and an injection of $R$ into its $a$-adic completion.
\end{corollary}

\section{The boundary: a domain where separation fails}

\begin{definition}
Let $S = \ZZ + X\,\QQ[X] = \{p \in \QQ[X] : p(0) \in \ZZ\}$, the subring of rational polynomials whose constant coefficient is an integer. Being a subring of the domain $\QQ[X]$, $S$ is a domain. Let $\varepsilon : S \to \QQ$, $\varepsilon(p) = p(0)$; its kernel is $X\QQ[X]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$2 \in S$ is nonzero and is not a unit of $S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $2b = 1$ in $S$, comparing constant coefficients gives $2m = 1$ for some $m \in \ZZ$, impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Xdiv}
For every $n$, $2^n \mid X$ in $S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$2^{-n} X$ has constant coefficient $0 \in \ZZ$, so $2^{-n}X \in S$, and $X = 2^n \cdot (2^{-n}X)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact computation of the intersection]\label{thm:counter}
In $S = \ZZ + X\QQ[X]$,
\[
  \bigcap_{n} (2^n) = X\,\QQ[X] = \ker \varepsilon = \{p \in S : p(0) = 0\}.
\]
In particular the principal filtration at $2$ is not separated.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\supseteq$) If $p \in S$ has $p(0) = 0$, then $2^{-n}p$ has constant coefficient $0$, so lies in $S$, and $p = 2^n(2^{-n}p)$.
($\subseteq$) Let $p \in \bigcap_n (2^n)$ and $m = p(0) \in \ZZ$. For each $n$ write $p = 2^n q_n$ with $q_n \in S$, and set $k_n = q_n(0) \in \ZZ$. Comparing constant coefficients gives $m = 2^n k_n$, so $2^n \mid m$ in $\ZZ$ for every $n$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:int}, $m = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The \emph{failure} of separation in $S$ is proved by \emph{invoking} separation in $\ZZ$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness]\label{cor:sharp}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Separation can fail at a nonzero non-unit: that hypothesis alone is insufficient in a general domain.
\item $S$ is not well founded for divisibility (as $X, X/2, X/4, \dots$ is an infinite properly descending divisibility chain), so the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:krull} cannot be dropped.
\item $S$ is neither Noetherian nor a unique factorization domain.
\item No height function for $2$ exists on $S$: for every $v : S \to \NN$ there is a nonzero $x \in S$ with $v(2x) \le v(x)$.
\item The greatest $2$-divisible ideal of $S$ is $X\QQ[X]$, by Theorems~\ref{thm:gfp} and~\ref{thm:counter}.
\item The $2$-adic topology on $S$ is not Hausdorff, and the map to the $2$-adic completion has kernel $X\QQ[X]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Why $S$ fails: an archimedean reading}

$S$ carries two independent scales. The constant coefficient lives in $\ZZ$, where dividing by $2$ costs one unit; the higher coefficients live in $\QQ$, where dividing by $2$ is free. The resulting value monoid at $2$ is lexicographically ordered of rank two --- informally $\ZZ \times \ZZ$ with $(1,0)$ recording powers of $2$ and $(0,1)$ the $X$-direction --- and a lexicographic order of rank $\ge 2$ is non-archimedean. Since $\NN$ \emph{is} archimedean, Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} says separation is exactly the condition that the relevant value monoid embeds order-compatibly in $\NN$.

\section{Strictly beyond the Noetherian hypothesis}\label{sec:beyond}

\begin{definition}
Let $A = \QQ[X_0, X_1, X_2, \dots]$ be the polynomial ring in countably many variables, and $\mathfrak{m} = (X_0, X_1, \dots)$ the irrelevant ideal.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$\mathfrak{m}$ is not finitely generated; hence $A$ is not Noetherian.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\mathfrak{m} = (t_1, \dots, t_r)$. Let $V$ be the finite set of variables occurring in the $t_i$ and choose $k$ with $X_k \notin V$. Consider the evaluation $g$ sending $X_k \mapsto 1$ and every other variable to $0$. Each $t_i$ involves only variables in $V$, all sent to $0$, so $g(t_i)$ is the constant coefficient of $t_i$, which is $0$ because $t_i \in \mathfrak{m}$. Hence $g$ annihilates $\mathfrak{m}$. But $X_k \in \mathfrak{m}$ and $g(X_k) = 1 \ne 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$A$ is a unique factorization domain, hence well founded for divisibility, hence every non-unit $a \in A$ has separated principal filtration. In particular $\bigcap_n (X_0^n) = 0$, and $\|\cdot\|_{X_0}$ is a non-archimedean absolute value on $A$ with $\|X_0\| = 1/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$A$ is the directed union of the UFDs $\QQ[X_0, \dots, X_N]$ and is itself a UFD; apply Theorems~\ref{thm:krull} and~\ref{thm:abs}, noting $X_0$ is prime.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Computing the adic order}
Given a ring with decidable divisibility by $a$ and a nonzero $x$, the order is computed by trial division: initialise $k \leftarrow 0$; while $a \mid x$, replace $x$ by $x/a$ and increment $k$; return $k$. Termination is exactly separation. In $\ZZ$ with $|a| \ge 2$ the loop runs $O(\log_{|a|}|x|)$ times; in $k[X]$ at $a = X$ at most $\deg x$ times.

\subsection{Certifying separation by exhibiting a height}
To certify $\Sep(a)$ for a concrete ring it suffices to exhibit a height function and verify $v(x) < v(ax)$ on nonzero $x$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} this is a complete method, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:min} the adic order is the tightest such $v$.

\subsection{Detecting failure}
To refute separation one exhibits a nonzero $a$-divisible ideal (Corollary~\ref{cor:gfp}). In the $D+M$ construction one exhibits $X$ together with the family $\{2^{-n}X\}$.

\subsection{Adic approximation}
Separation guarantees the residues $x \bmod (a^n)$ determine $x$; distinct elements are separated at level $\ord_a(x-y)+1$, so equality testing terminates. Without separation no such guarantee exists.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{$p$-adic numbers and local fields.} Theorem~\ref{thm:abs} applied to $(\ZZ, p)$ gives the $p$-adic absolute value; Theorem~\ref{thm:compl} says $\ZZ \hookrightarrow \ZZ_p$ is injective.

\paragraph{Formal power series and local geometry.} Applied to $(k[X], X)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:compl} gives $k[X] \hookrightarrow k[[X]]$. The order of vanishing is the height, and Theorem~\ref{thm:min} says it is the sharpest notion of ``how strongly a function vanishes''.

\paragraph{Krull's intersection theorem.} Corollary~\ref{cor:noeth} is the principal case of the Krull intersection theorem; Theorem~\ref{thm:krull} extends it beyond Noetherian rings.

\paragraph{Non-Noetherian adic analysis.} Section~\ref{sec:beyond} provides a non-Noetherian ring carrying a genuine non-archimedean absolute value.

\paragraph{A diagnostic for pathological rings.} Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp} turns a single divisibility computation into simultaneous proofs that a ring is non-Noetherian, non-factorial, non-well-founded, and carries no compatible $\NN$-valued height.

\paragraph{Termination of division algorithms.} Separation is the exact correctness condition for the ``divide out all factors of $a$'' loop occurring in factorisation and normalisation routines.

\section{Discussion and open directions}

\subsection{What the theory says}
Three slogans summarise the development. (i) \emph{Separation is a height statement, not a chain statement} (Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}). (ii) \emph{Separation is triviality of a greatest fixed point} (Theorem~\ref{thm:gfp}). (iii) \emph{Separation is an archimedean axiom} (Section~8.1).

\subsection{The height spectrum of a domain}
For a domain $R$ define $\Sep(R) = \{a \in R : \bigcap_n (a^n) = 0\}$. This set contains $0$; misses every unit (in a nontrivial ring); is upward closed under divisibility (if $a \mid b$ and $a \in \Sep(R)$ then $b \in \Sep(R)$); hence is closed under multiplication by arbitrary elements and under positive powers; is invariant under multiplication by units; and pulls back along injective ring homomorphisms, so that separation for an element of a subring can be read off from the ambient ring. In a domain well founded for divisibility, Theorem~\ref{thm:char} says $\Sep(R)$ is exactly the complement of the unit group.

\begin{problem}[Height spectrum]
Characterise the domains for which $\Sep(R)$ equals the complement of the units. Conjecturally this holds iff $R$ has no nonzero ideal $I$ with $I = aI$ for some non-unit $a$, and further iff every localisation of $R$ at a height-one prime has archimedean value group.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Rank-two obstruction]
Prove that for a domain $R$ and a nonzero non-unit $a$, the failure $\bigcap_n(a^n) \ne 0$ forces a rank-$\ge 2$ lexicographic valuation-theoretic structure adapted to $a$, making the $D+M$ example the universal obstruction.
\end{problem}

\subsection{Further directions}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Non-principal filtrations.} Does a height-style criterion exist for $\bigcap_n I^n$ with $I$ arbitrary? The absence of a single multiplier complicates the induction of Lemma~\ref{lem:growth}.
\item \textbf{Ordinal-valued heights.} Replacing $\NN$ by a well-ordered set should track the rank of the obstruction; lexicographic $\ZZ^n$ heights are the natural candidates.
\item \textbf{Quantitative separation.} How large can $\ord_a(x)$ be relative to natural size measures? In $\ZZ$, $\ord_a(x) \le \log_{|a|}|x|$.
\item \textbf{Module-theoretic version.} When is $\bigcap_n a^n M = 0$? The fixed-point description generalises verbatim; the height criterion needs torsion-freeness in place of the domain hypothesis.
\item \textbf{Effective non-existence of heights.} Can one, for a given $v$ on $\ZZ + X\QQ[X]$, effectively produce a witness $x$ with $v(2x) \le v(x)$?
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Starting from $\bigcap_n (2^n) = 0$ in $\ZZ$, we isolated the separation property of a principal filtration and gave five equivalent descriptions of it over a domain: finiteness of all $a$-multiplicities; existence of an $\NN$-valued height increasing under multiplication by $a$; triviality of the greatest $a$-divisible ideal; Hausdorffness of the $a$-adic topology; and injectivity into the $a$-adic completion. Chain conditions are sufficient rather than necessary, and their role is to manufacture a height; the $a$-adic order is then the canonical minimal such height, and for prime $a$ it exponentiates to a non-archimedean absolute value. The ring $\ZZ + X\QQ[X]$ marks the precise boundary, with $\bigcap_n (2^n) = X\QQ[X]$ computed exactly, while $\QQ[X_0, X_1, \dots]$ shows that the chain-condition formulation reaches genuinely beyond the Noetherian world. The unifying moral is that repeated division terminates exactly when the ring's scale of measurement is archimedean.

